  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 222 AM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nPrecipitation Trends...A narrow line of showers and storms was \nmoving east across the forecast area late last night into early this \nmorning ahead of a cold front. These elevated storms are being \nsupported by MLCAPE between 500-1000 J/kg and effective shear around \n30 kts and should remain non-severe through the remainder of the \nmorning, but brief heavy downpours will be associated with any of \nthe showers or storms. These showers and storms will move out of the \narea by sunrise. Cyclonic flow from a mid-level trough/shortwave \nwill trail behind the front and generate scattered showers this \nafternoon aided by daytime heating. A couple hundred J/kg of CAPE \nwill be also be present during the afternoon, which may generate a \nfew rumbles of thunder at times. Inverted-V soundings would also \nsupport gusty winds within the heavier showers/storms. The shower \nactivity will come to an end by this evening.\n\nBesides a very low chance of showers (up to 20%) in far northeast WI \nearly Thursday evening from a weak backdoor cold front, dry \nconditions are expected for Thursday and Friday. The next chance for \nshowers and storms arrives this weekend into early next week with \nthe potential for a couple thunderstorm complexes, but location and \ntiming details remain uncertain. Will continue to monitor the \npotential for severe weather as the synoptic pattern and long-\nrange AI models are favoring severe weather occurring across the \nUpper Midwest at some point.\n\nTemperatures...Throughout the week and into the beginning of the \nweekend, temperatures, both highs and lows, will hover around \nnormal. Highs will mainly be in the 70s to low 80s, with lows \ngenerally in the 50s to low 60s. A few locations across the \nNorthwoods could see lows in the upper 30s to mid 40s Thursday night \ninto Friday morning. If these values trend colder, a few spots may \nsee some frost into Friday morning. Late this weekend into early \nnext week, temperatures will warm above normal with highs in the low \nto mid 80s, and dew points increasing into the 60s.",
